            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did mātramē mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: అయితే",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "అయితే (ayitē) — \"in that case\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "అయితే (ayitē) picks up what the other person has just said and turns on it: సరే, అయితే (ayitē) వెళ్ళొస్తాను — \"alright, in that case I'll be off\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is put together out of the piece Telugu uses for \"it happened\" plus the little ending that means \"if\", so what it literally says is \"if it is so\". English \"well then\" and \"in that case\" both land in the same spot."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "At the head of an answer, on its own, అయితే (ayitē) is one of the most useful things a learner can carry. It buys a moment, and it shows the other person you were listening."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
