        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: తరువాత",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "తరువాత (taruvāta) — \"after that, later\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "తరువాత puts two things in order: bhōjanaṁ taruvāta, \"after the meal\". It goes after the thing it follows, exactly like కూడా (kūḍā) and మాత్రమే (mātramē) before it — a habit worth noticing, because it holds for a great many of these small words."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The word is inherited. Standing alone at the front of a sentence it means \"later\", and it is the gentle way to put something off: తరువాత (taruvāta), not now, and no reason offered."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "తరువాత (taruvāta) is also how a story gets kept moving. A listener who says it is asking you to go on."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four, and this one is about order."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
