            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: what did taruvāta mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ఇంకా",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ఇంకా (iṅkā) — \"still, yet\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ఇంకా (iṅkā) says the thing is not finished. ఇంకా (iṅkā) ఉంది is \"there is still some\"; ఇంకా (iṅkā) రాలేదు is \"she hasn't come yet\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is the natural answer to అంతే. Where అంతే closes a list off, ఇంకా (iṅkā) says there is more of it coming, and the two make a pair the way కూడా (kūḍā) and మాత్రమే (mātramē) do."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five small words, and not one of them names a thing. కూడా (kūḍā), మాత్రమే (mātramē), అయితే (ayitē), తరువాత (taruvāta), ఇంకా (iṅkā) are what turn a set of separate answers into a conversation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five: కూడా (kūḍā), మాత్రమే (mātramē), అయితే (ayitē), తరువాత (taruvāta), ఇంకా (iṅkā). Enough to add to an answer, limit it, order it, or keep it open."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
